<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Taranto</h1><p class="st-meta">1088 CE – 1463 CE (approx.) · capital Taranto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A great southern Italian fief created for the crusader Bohemond, later held by princes so powerful they rivalled the kings of Naples.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard's</a> death the Norman inheritance was divided, and in 1088 his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond</a> received Taranto and much of the heel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> as compensation for losing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a> to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-borsa">Roger Borsa</a>. Bohemond soon left on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-antioch">Principality of Antioch</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Taranto became a fief within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples</a>, often granted to royal relatives, such as the Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-of-taranto">Philip I of Taranto</a> from 1294. Its princes controlled the ports and olive and grain lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and could field large armies. Under the Orsini del Balzo in the 15th century it covered much of southern Apulia.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-antonio-orsini-del-balzo">Giovanni Antonio Orsini del Balzo</a>, who rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">King Ferrante</a>, died in 1463 without legitimate heirs. Ferrante seized his lands, and the principality was absorbed into the royal domain of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-taranto.jpg" alt="Principality of Taranto" width="320" height="364"><figcaption>Image: My own edits, upon original work by MapMapster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principato_di_Taranto_nel_Regno_di_Sicilia,_1154.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1088 CE – 1463 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Taranto</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bohemond I of Antioch, Philip I of Taranto, Giovanni Antonio Orsini del Balzo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italo-Romance, Greek, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Taranto</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=principality-of-taranto" aria-label="Principality of Taranto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5103 732 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Taranto lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5423 988L5333 1064M5423 988L5349 976"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5333 1064h0M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5423 988h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5423 988h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1276&amp;focus=principality-of-taranto">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
